From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 18 Jun 2026 18:30:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 02645/11362] [outer-loop] tr87 glyph verifier: Hamming-nearest
 attempt + sharper boundary (3 remaining perception steps)

Second grounding attempt (Hamming-nearest to the rule codebook, tolerant glyph matching) also does not
ground, and reveals the gap is deeper than tolerant clustering. Three remaining perception unknowns, each
sidestepped by the adapter's internal-state read: (1) glyph-value identity needs a learned 7-way
classifier (same value's bitmaps are far enough apart that Hamming collapses distinct glyphs); (2)
target-region identity is itself uncertain from pixels (rows 41-45 match the rule LHS poorly -> probably
not the target series); (3) the win is a greedy MULTI-GLYPH SEQUENCE rewrite (adapter _required_editable),
not a position-wise substitution, so it needs the sequence-rewrite engine even with perfect glyph ids.

Banked: the reusable segment_glyphs primitive + the rule-prototype decode (both work). Honest verdict
unchanged: complete_glyph_pixel_decode_partial_not_grounded. Pragmatic note: tr87 already has a working
internal-state verifier (the adapter); the from-pixels version is a first-contact/sovereignty upgrade,
not a correctness gap. Adversarial-verify clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
